
I was hired as an IC2 Software Developer. The Technical interview was split into three parts. first, they asked about my background and experience with the technologies I'd be using. In the second part, they asked a few direct technical questions and also discussed how I would approach solving certain problems. The last part was a coding challenge on HackerRank, where I had to solve a real-world problem.

You'll likely face medium-difficulty coding problems that test your problem-solving approach and code quality, plus questions about distributed systems basics, database concepts, and cloud architecture since it's OCI. The interviewer wants to see how you think through problems and communicate your reasoning, not just whether you can memorize algorithms.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Oracle interviews can be unpredictable depending on your interviewer's background, so you need to be ready for anything from data structures questions to discussing scalability trade-offs. Focus on being able to explain your thought process clearly and ask clarifying questions when given a problem. Practice coding out loud and be prepared to discuss your past projects in technical detail, especially any experience with distributed systems or cloud technologies. I'm on the team that built interview copilot, and it's designed exactly for situations like this where you need to navigate those curveball technical questions and articulate your thinking process confidently during the actual interview.

